WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.352 --> 00:00:06.810
DEMMA ROSA RODRIGUEZ:
Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.360
I'm Demma Rodriguez.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.463
I'm the Head of Equity
Engineering at Google.

00:00:10.463 --> 00:00:12.380
And today, I'll be talking
to you a little bit

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.160
about a team I started called
Equity Engineering, the impact

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.640
and the opportunity of
designing for Google's most

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.395
vulnerable users first.

00:00:21.395 --> 00:00:23.640
I'm really excited
to talk to you today.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.285
And thank you so much
for being here with us.

00:00:26.285 --> 00:00:28.660
Before we get started, let's
talk a little bit about what

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.120
Equity Engineering is.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.760
Equity Engineering is a
comprehensive approach

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.320
to building equitable products
for underrepresented and future

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.520
users.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:40.630
And what does that mean?

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.060
When I say "equity,"
oftentimes people

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.080
wonder if I'm talking
about the stock

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.670
market or financial products.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:53.590
But really, equity is a
concept and has a definition

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:58.210
of providing the tools and
services and opportunities

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.640
to different users
that they need in order

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:07.370
to make sure that they have
outcomes that suit their needs

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:10.150
but also that meet
them where they are.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.340
So working across
different communities,

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:15.490
what are the tools and
services and perspectives

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.800
that need to be understood
in order to make

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.280
sure that we are building
products and services that

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.510
help all of our users and
don't advantage some users

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.410
to the disadvantage of others?

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.110
But more about that in a minute.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:32.260
Let's get through
the agenda and talk

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.170
about how we're going to
walk through this pretty

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:36.912
complex topic.

00:01:36.912 --> 00:01:38.620
So the first thing
that we're going to do

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.560
is talk about, what
are our pillars?

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.350
What are the guideposts
that we use in order

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.090
to frame this work?

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.970
Then we're going to
step into our goals.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:52.280
Fundamentally, what
are we focused on?

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.840
How do we do it?

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.190
What is the model that we use
in order to get the work done?

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.700
And what is our approach
in continuing to advance

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.020
this work across Google?

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.600
Lastly, we'll talk
about the impact, what

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.960
are some areas of
opportunity, and some traction

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.750
that we've made.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.000
What is our moonshot?

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.950
And by the way,
what is a moonshot?

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.800
What do Googlers mean
when they say that?

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.440
And then we'll talk
about some key takeaways

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.560
that you can incorporate
into your own practice,

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:30.730
into your teams, and
hopefully into your company.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:35.760
So Equity Engineering--
some fast facts about us.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.390
Equity engineering is a
multidomain, cross-product-area

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:44.370
effort that evaluates
processes, consumer products,

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.560
and internal systems.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.340
We facilitate more equitable
experiences and outcomes

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.990
for users through process
experimentation in partnership

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:58.545
with the Chief Diversity Office
and all applicable product

00:02:58.545 --> 00:02:59.045
areas.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:06.580
We really have some
lofty guideposts.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.620
The first one is we want
to transform development.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:13.040
We want to infuse a mindset
of diversity, equity,

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.870
and inclusion throughout product
development and engineering

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:18.200
at Google.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.128
And we have a specific theory
of change of where to start.

00:03:21.128 --> 00:03:23.170
So we'll talk about that
in a little bit as well.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.930
How do we know where to start?

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.740
How do we do this work?

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.130
We want to centralize research.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:37.140
We really believe that ethical
user experience research

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.620
that is focused on constructing
user journeys and product

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.030
roadmaps that directly
impact underrepresented

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:49.620
or marginalized groups is where
we begin as priority zero.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:52.980
We also want to make sure
that we're building capacity.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.550
We want other teams
and other technologists

00:03:56.550 --> 00:04:00.130
to understand how to approach
this work independently.

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:01.890
So we teach and
develop technologists

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:06.690
to understand and prioritize
use cases that negatively impact

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.890
underrepresented users.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:12.270
We also understand that
underrepresented users

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.180
look and act differently
throughout the world.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.399
As a multinational
organization, we

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:21.120
know that we have different
tools and different research

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.100
for different places
around the world.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.490
We really work on
being multiethnic,

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:29.400
multinational,
cross-perspective in order

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.560
to include as many
users as possible.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:40.000
So we really are beginning as a
horizontal team with scope that

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.260
focuses on HR
first-party tools, tools

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.660
that are built by
Google for Google,

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:48.610
and third-party
tools, tools that

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:50.350
are used across the
industry that we

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.450
have incorporated at Google.

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:56.600
Now, I'm sure you're wondering,
why start with HR first?

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.120
Well, at Google, our hiring
and professional development

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.600
processes are very
tailored to the way

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.590
that Google does business.

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:05.020
When I started
this team, I wanted

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.810
to focus on accelerating
Google's goals to build

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:10.380
a more diverse workplace first.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.030
It's a core
philosophy that I hold

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.460
and that I wanted to
bring into the team

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:20.470
around cleaning up
your own home first.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:23.830
Our theory of change is that
as we bring in more people

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:26.500
from all walks of
life, our products

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.420
will be better
and more inclusive

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:31.120
by default if
their creators come

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.220
from more places
around the world,

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:35.440
from different
socioeconomic classes,

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.690
from different
religions, perspectives,

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:39.470
and walks of life.

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:42.650
Therefore we want to start
with the most pressing issue.

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:44.650
How do we make
Google a workplace

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:46.630
that reflects the
beautiful variability

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:49.870
and diversity of our
communities around the world?

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:52.050
This is a core
value and something

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.990
that differentiates our work
from other products inclusion

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:59.870
initiatives that seek to
educate the existing workplace.

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:03.170
While education is an
important tenet of our mission,

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:07.440
equitable hiring, progression,
and inclusive culture

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.230
are our first priority.

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:14.810
Secondly, we want to scale
our impact by joining forces.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.720
We want everyone at the table.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.090
We want to make sure that Google
program managers, engineers,

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.860
product managers, UX
researchers across the company

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.890
have an opportunity
to really focus

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:31.700
on how we can advance these
goals, internally first,

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:34.550
and then more broadly
throughout the industry

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:37.380
and throughout the world.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.570
We also make sure that
each of our partners

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:43.260
are dedicated to the development
of products and systems

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:45.870
that work for our
users, especially

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:47.550
our most vulnerable users.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:51.030
We want to drive parity
across those experiences.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:53.700
And taking a quick step
back, driving parity

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:57.120
means we want to make sure
that our users who are enjoying

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:01.710
the most benefits, who feel
the most psychologically safe,

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:06.060
and who are able to really
advance their careers--

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.880
that there is not a delta
between their experiences

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.140
and other groups.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:13.410
We want our groups that are
having a more difficult time

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:18.170
to have an easier go at building
the best careers that they can

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:22.540
at Google, at being
progressed, and being valued.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:26.400
And so therefore our goal is
really to make sure that first

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.740
within Google and then
throughout the industry

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.350
and for all of our
users that everyone

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:37.320
has the same opportunity and
the same outcomes in how they're

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:42.750
able to enjoy a career at
Google, to enjoy our products,

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:46.350
and also to benefit
from the prosperity that

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:49.860
comes from technology
and the sector at large.

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:53.960
That was a lot.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:56.870
And definitely, those
goals seem pretty good.

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:58.880
Sounds really good
on paper, right?

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:00.590
But how do we get this done?

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:04.820
Well, we get it done with
a theoretical framework,

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:09.170
a partnership model that
helps teams understand

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:11.120
how they can be successful.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.430
What are their core
responsibilities?

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:15.980
But where are the
opportunities to collaborate?

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:19.100
So from the key performance
indicators side,

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:22.620
what the opportunity assessments
are with diversity, equity,

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.590
and inclusion, we think
it's really important

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:27.570
to defer to the teams that
have expertise in this area.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:29.570
And so in that regard,
we partner really closely

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:31.550
with the Chief Diversity
Office to make sure

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:34.520
that we understand, what
is Google's broad diversity

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:35.820
strategy?

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:37.580
What is the prioritization?

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:39.890
And also, what are the
opportunity assessments

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:41.630
that we can carry forward?

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:44.390
We then help to
drive cultural change

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:47.420
by creating pilots,
training, and documentation

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:51.080
for our technologists in order
to help inform the product

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:54.590
strategy, the area
priorities, and team

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:57.200
objectives and key results.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:59.960
And then lastly, we
empower all of the teams

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:03.740
across the Diversity Office
and also product teams

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:07.610
by providing consultations and
referrals, either internally

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:10.430
or externally to
experts in industry.

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:13.850
We help with prototypes
and experiments

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:17.390
so that we can innovate in
ways that help us to create

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:20.280
rapid opportunity assessments.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:24.650
And we do reviews for existing
products that are in flight

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:26.870
and ready to be
produced or produced

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:30.900
and ready to launch
within Google.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:34.400
So now let's talk a little bit
about the collaboration model

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:39.950
and how it's driven so much
impact here across our company.

00:09:39.950 --> 00:09:43.350
To date-- and it's really
difficult to process.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:45.930
But to date, just over
the last 18 months,

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:48.840
we have worked across
72 different teams

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:50.370
across Alphabet.

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:51.870
Oftentimes an
issue that we think

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:54.610
only impacts Google really
impacts multiple of our bets.

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:56.700
And so we've had the
pleasure of really working

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:00.130
across the entire
Alphabet community.

00:10:00.130 --> 00:10:03.780
And we've done that by helping
to elevate sensitive use cases.

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:06.498
Oftentimes when we're thinking
about marginalized users,

00:10:06.498 --> 00:10:08.790
we really want to make sure
that we take the burden off

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:11.280
of those communities
from having to describe

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:15.070
their own challenges with our
processes and our products.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:17.940
And so we hope to elevate
those sensitive use cases

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:22.540
and help to protect users from
further psychological harm.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:25.350
We also help to
inform process design.

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:27.330
So oftentimes in
engineering, it's

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:30.570
not just thinking about
what the tools do.

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:33.630
But it's also thinking about,
what are the processes?

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:37.620
How do we create a professional
development paradigm

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:40.440
where users from all
different kinds of backgrounds

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:42.330
can learn and grow
and understand

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.390
what their next steps are?

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:47.090
That then informs
our product design.

00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:49.527
For a lot of Google's
processes, as we said,

00:10:49.527 --> 00:10:51.860
it's really unique to the way
that Google does business.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:54.860
And so therefore our products
can be quite unique as well.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:57.230
Working product and
process together

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:00.710
helps drive mindful
innovation that focuses

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:03.720
on our underrepresented users.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:05.760
We also then help with launches.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:08.900
As we're getting ready to deploy
these tools and processes,

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:11.220
are we thinking about
all of the right things?

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:13.950
Do we have all our checks
and balances in place?

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:16.760
And then lastly, even once
something is launched,

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:18.950
there's a lot of change
management that comes along.

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:21.393
And so our program managers
help with change management

00:11:21.393 --> 00:11:23.060
across the teams that
we're working with

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:26.150
to help lighten that load.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:28.127
What we found as we
dug across our company

00:11:28.127 --> 00:11:29.960
is that there were many
opportunities for us

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.043
to work with different
teams on improving products

00:11:32.043 --> 00:11:33.110
and processes.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:37.060
We have identified
over 159 improvements.

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:40.530
81% of those have
already launched.

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:42.530
And we know that we're
on track to make progress

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:45.410
in 2020, which is really
rewarding considering

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:47.480
all the challenges that
this year has brought.

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:50.090
We see ourselves moving this
work forward and helping

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:52.610
to improve the space
just a little bit,

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:56.310
even through some of
the hardest times.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:58.470
We want to make sure that
our growth is sustainable

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:01.230
and that it can be-- and that
it is, in fact, sustained.

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:03.570
And so our resourcing
in 2020 and moving

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:07.650
forward is around distribution
across multiple disciplines.

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:10.800
Here at Google, we call
those disciplines "ladders."

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:15.190
And it essentially refers to
different domains of expertise.

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:19.020
And so right now, we're working
with user experience, program

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:23.400
management, product management,
and software engineering.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:25.160
We believe that a
cross-functional and

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:27.170
cross-organizational
model is going

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:28.717
to help our work to scale.

00:12:28.717 --> 00:12:30.300
And it's going to
help everyone invest

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.550
in this very important work.

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:34.840
We also know that
specificity matters.

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:39.510
And so we maintain a core
working group of 10 specialists

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:41.760
that really think
about equity cases

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:45.000
across multiple topics
that impact our company

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.190
and industry.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:49.520
So back to this moonshot.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:51.530
What does it mean
when Googlers say,

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:55.500
we are aiming towards a
moonshot, or our moonshot?

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:59.180
A moonshot is a Google term for
any big, long-term, expensive

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:03.080
project to solve an extremely
challenging problem that

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:05.690
has a chance of failing
but, if we succeed,

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:08.233
could fundamentally
change society.

00:13:08.233 --> 00:13:09.650
And "expensive"
can mean anything.

00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:11.120
It can mean time.

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:13.350
It can mean sweat equity.

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:16.520
It can mean financial
resources or prioritization.

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:19.460
But the term alludes
to, fundamentally,

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:24.230
this race between 1955 to 1972
to race to the moon as part

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:25.790
of the Apollo program.

00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:27.680
Many said it wasn't possible.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:30.320
There were many
failures along the way.

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:33.770
And the path towards progress
certainly wasn't linear.

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:35.970
But it was definitely
worthwhile.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:40.760
And so our moonshot is just as
lofty and just as challenging.

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:43.820
We believe that
structural discrimination

00:13:43.820 --> 00:13:46.910
is a form of institutional
discrimination.

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:52.850
And we know that that has a
real impact in engineering.

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:56.450
We understand that systemic bias
is an unintended outcome that

00:13:56.450 --> 00:13:59.570
results from a lack of
representation of women, older

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:03.260
workers, Black and
Latinx people, nonbinary

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:04.920
and transgender people.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:09.590
But we do believe that we can
dismantle the systemic bias

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:11.190
in engineering globally.

00:14:11.190 --> 00:14:14.900
And that's really
the core moonshot.

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:18.020
We want to empower and drive
access to the tech industry

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:19.010
at large.

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:22.490
We understand that in the
rarefied air of companies

00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:25.310
like Google, there
are many people who

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:27.830
never get an opportunity
to have their voices heard.

00:14:27.830 --> 00:14:30.920
And we want to make our
company and our industry

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:34.280
more accessible to those people,
not just in the United States,

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:36.060
but all over the world.

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:38.960
We want to then share our
findings in the open source

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:39.810
community.

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:43.250
We want to promote universal
equitable frameworks.

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:45.980
Because this is really about
improvement and effecting

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:51.950
change, not just for ourselves,
but to companies like us,

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:54.920
to the industry, and to
users and people at large.

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:01.785
With that, what can we
do to effect change?

00:15:01.785 --> 00:15:03.660
And since I'm sharing
this with you, what can

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:05.932
you do to effect change?

00:15:05.932 --> 00:15:07.890
The first thing that I'm
going to ask you to do

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:09.980
is to take a hard
look in the mirror.

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:13.860
At Google, we have the brand
slogan "build for everyone."

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:16.140
But how can we actually
build for everyone

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:18.600
when we do not have a
representative workforce

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:22.350
or engagement model that
centralize the community's

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:23.725
feedback first?

00:15:23.725 --> 00:15:26.230
The truth is we can't.

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:30.530
We have to really focus on
our most vulnerable users.

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:34.420
We have to be aware of the
ways that our products can

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:37.550
either advantage or
disadvantage groups.

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:40.170
And we have to start thinking
about our most vulnerable users

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:40.670
first.

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:46.470
With that, we know that we
can't build for everyone.

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:49.200
We are building on
behalf of everyone.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:53.170
We want to build with everyone.

00:15:53.170 --> 00:15:55.900
The work cannot
happen in a vacuum.

00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:58.030
And it certainly does
not happen or will not

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:00.820
happen when the technology
sector as a whole

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:03.340
is still failing at
representation and hiring

00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:05.270
and in retention.

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:09.350
That said, we're also not
going to pack up and go home.

00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:11.710
So how do we build for everyone?

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:14.170
We build with our users.

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:17.350
We need to engage our
users across the spectrum

00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:21.570
of humanity, engage our users
that are the least like us,

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:24.310
and be intentional
about centralizing them

00:16:24.310 --> 00:16:27.850
first, not as an afterthought.

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:29.620
We also want to design
for the user that

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:32.650
has the most difficulty
accessing the product.

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:35.080
Building for those who
have additional challenges

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:38.050
are going to make our
product better for everyone.

00:16:38.050 --> 00:16:39.490
Another way of
thinking about this

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:44.260
is never trade off accessibility
or any other equity

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:48.750
issue for short-term velocity.

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:54.630
Measure once, measure twice,
three times before you cut--

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:57.000
good, sound practice.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.190
Measure equity
throughout your systems.

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:00.600
Do not assume it.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:05.280
Recognize that your decision
makers are also subject to bias

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:08.400
and can be undereducated
in the causes of inequity.

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:11.710
So therefore you need a
mixed-method research.

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:14.760
You may not have the expertise
to identify or measure

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:17.190
the scope of an equity issue.

00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:18.760
That's OK.

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:21.369
A lot of us weren't
trained to understand this.

00:17:21.369 --> 00:17:23.970
And so while we're not
responsible for the lack

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:28.420
of knowledge, we are accountable
to gaining the information.

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:31.680
Remember that if you cater
to a single user base,

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:34.290
you may be
disenfranchising another.

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:38.920
These trade-offs can be hard to
spot and impossible to reverse.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:40.260
So be proactive.

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:42.720
Partner with teams and
individuals that are subject

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:45.460
matter experts in diversity,
equity, and inclusion.

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:47.610
And don't be scared to
go outside of your team

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:49.110
or even outside of your company.

00:17:51.850 --> 00:17:53.310
It's a big task.

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:54.490
It's a hard one.

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:57.793
It is most definitely a
moonshot, if not many.

00:17:57.793 --> 00:17:59.460
But I want to leave
you with the thought

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:01.320
that change is possible.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:04.470
The problems that we're
facing with technology today,

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:08.010
everything from surveillance
to disinformation

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:11.700
to online harassment, are
genuinely overwhelming.

00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:15.330
And it's OK if you have
moments of feeling overwhelmed.

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:19.200
We're not going to solve
these problems overnight.

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:21.720
But we're also not going to
solve with the same failed

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:25.900
approaches of the past or just
the skills we already have.

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:27.960
So take a moment to
think about, what

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:31.440
are the tools,
services, and skills

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:33.720
that have gotten us here?

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:35.010
Meditate on that.

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:39.000
But then further think, what are
the tools, skills, and services

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:43.770
that we need to get to the next
iteration of what we would like

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:46.560
technology to be and
how we would like

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:48.750
humans to access these tools?

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:52.830
And who gets to be part of not
just the prosperity, but also

00:18:52.830 --> 00:18:56.220
of the development of the tools
and services that help people

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:58.650
all over the world?

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:00.390
We know that we need to change.

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:02.630
And I want to leave
you with the thought

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:04.430
that it's possible
for us to change

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:06.157
if we all get in together.

00:19:06.157 --> 00:19:07.490
Thank you so much for your time.

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:11.650
And thank you about learning
about Equity Engineering.

